      "question": {
        ":question/id": "0d6985ec-3500-52de-993a-80591e452c25",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
        ":question/problem": "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{6}\\right),\\qquad y=\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right).\n$$\n\nConsider the following table of values. Angles are in radians.\n\n| **$t$** | $-4$ | $-3$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $a$ | $-1$ | $b$ |\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $0$ |\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $c$ |\n\nFind the exact value of $a-b+c$.\n\n{{result}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The $a$ entry is in the $x$-row under $t=-4$. Substitute into $x=\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{6}\\right)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a &=\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi \\left(-4\\right)}{6}\\right) \\\\ &=\\sin\\left(- \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $b$ entry is in the $x$-row under $t=-2$. Substitute into $x=\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{6}\\right)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b &=\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi \\left(-2\\right)}{6}\\right) \\\\ &=\\sin\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $c$ entry is in the $y$-row under $t=1$. Substitute into $y=\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c &=\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi \\left(1\\right)}{4}\\right) \\\\ &=\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the three entries with the signs in the requested expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}a-b+c \\\\ =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nThe completed table is\n\n| **$t$** | $-4$ | $-3$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $-1$ | $- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ |\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $0$ |\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |",
